From caf-talk Caf Feb  3 12:57:26 1993
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,comp.org.eff.talk,alt.privacy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: jkp@cs.HUT.FI (Jyrki Kuoppala)
Subject: Article 1--Logging anon. ftp usage
Message-ID: <1993Feb3.103442.12179@nntp.hut.fi>
Date: Wed, 3 Feb 1993 10:34:42 GMT

Many anon. ftp servers currently log the host name&address,
ident/password and file transfers of the "anon" users, with no stated
policy on what to use the logs for.

What's the net.wisdom on this?

Is it only right and proper, and necessary to catch all the child
molesters, drug pushers and cryptojunkies and report their identities
to the proper authorities?

Or a case of "it's my ftp server, my property and I'll log whatever I
damn please and do whatever I want with the info - you're free to not
connect"?

What about when the ftp server is public property, ie. funded by
taxpayers?  Should the logs be a matter of public record to account
for the use of the taxpayer money, or just cheap food for the
respective domestic intelligence agencies' profile gathering
activities?

Is logging a case of "the fascist big brother should have no business
invading my privacy, and it's probably illegal collection of personal
information in many countries"?

Should we start keeping a list of "the bad guys" keeping ftp servers
(who don't log the identities, or who do look the id's, depending on
your answers to previous questions) and start badmouthing/boycotting
them?  Should ftp maintainers/users/uploaders take personal
responsibility and stand on the issue and decline to cooperate with
sites who log/don't log information?

What is the meaning of life?

//Jyrki


