From caf-talk Caf Feb  3 21:03:17 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: ldg@scott.skidmore.edu (Leo D. Geoffrion)
Subject: Article 7--an unusual privacy/rights question
Message-ID: <1993Feb4.013614.25163@scott.skidmore.edu>
Date: Thu, 4 Feb 1993 01:36:14 GMT

We have an unusual situation that has several of our administrator's
puzzled.

We issued a user account to a summer school student.  At the end of
the summer, that student left the college but continues to reside in
the local area.  Our normal practice is to purge such accounts from
the system at the end of the summer school, but by oversight this
account was missed.

Since then, this account appears to have been used for suspicipious
activities, including password cracking and other mischief.  To this
point, we've limited our search to open information such as accounting
logs and file names without looking at the contents of the files
themselves.

We've disabled the account since it was not supposed to be valid
anyway since the person is no longer affiliated with the college.  
Following standard practice, we've consulted with the various campus
disciplinary committees, but they all state that they have no
jurisdiction since this person is no longer a student or employee.
They feel they have no authority to authorize (or disapprove)
examination of the files in order to confirm illicit activity.

We've been cooperating with the police and may ask them to issue a
search warrant, but we're very curious about how far we can go in
examining the files without a formal warrant.  Simply put, what
privacy rights does an invalid account have?  Since it does not belong
there in the first place, what protections should it have?

If this person were a current student, we'd have procedures in place
to present our suspicions to the student integrity board who would
listen to any counter objections from the student and decide whether
to permit further search of the user files.  
-- 
